4. **Visual/manual** (proves the M3 card + live speed/ETA): with dev running (`bun run dev`), in a terminal pane drag-drop a multi-MB file → a bottom-right M3 card shows a determinate `md-linear-progress`, bytes/%, speed, and "N s left"; on completion it flips to "Uploaded" ✓ and auto-dismisses. Drop the **same file again** → card briefly shows "Checking…" then "Already uploaded · reused" ✓ (no upload progress). Cmd-click a large file path in the terminal → a "↓" card shows the same progress/speed/ETA, then the browser download fires.

## Assumptions & contingencies
- **Dedup scope is per-session** (the attachment dir is already per-session). If cross-session reuse is wanted later, promote the manifest to a worker-global file — but that complicates reaper interaction and cross-dir path validity, so it is deliberately out of scope now.
- **Client-side hash cap = 64 MiB.** Files above it upload without a dedup probe (browser can't hash multi-GB without breaking O(chunk) memory). If large-file dedup matters, the fallback is: keep the cap but have the worker's on-save hash enable a *server-observed* dedup on a subsequent identical upload via a cheap client pre-probe using `(size + first+last 1 MiB hash)` as the probe key — do **not** attempt full client hashing of huge files.
- **SHA-256 only, never filename.** A content match returns the stored path regardless of the new file's name; different content with the same name misses and overwrites (manifest self-heals via prune-by-value).
- **`md-linear-progress` import path** is `@material/web/progress/linear-progress.js` (standard `@material/web` export, same package already supplying `md-input-chip`). If that subpath is absent in the installed version, fall back to the existing hand-rolled 3px track (`AttachmentChip.tsx` L58–61) styled with `--md-sys-color-primary`, keeping the rest of the card unchanged.
- **Blob-in-memory downloads** are retained (no File System Access API streaming) — matches current behavior; only the transport is chunked. If browser memory on huge downloads becomes a problem, the follow-up is `showSaveFilePicker()` + a `WritableStream`, which is a separate change.
